 Good Friday afternoon. Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 623 words, a 2½-min. read. Thanks to Sheryl Miller for copy editing . 

 🇺🇸 Today we're remembering the nearly 3,000 lives lost 25 years ago on Sept. 11, 2001.

 🍯 Shanah Tovah to those celebrating Rosh Hashanah, the Jewish New Year, beginning at sundown. 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Inflation stays hot 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Bureau of Labor Statistics . Chart: Courtenay Brown/Axios Consumer prices stayed high in August as the Iran war's energy shock fueled a fresh burst of inflation, Axios' Courtenay Brown reports. 

 That's squeezing household budgets, with signs that underlying inflation is proving stubborn across the economy. By the numbers: The Consumer Price Index rose 0.4% in August from the previous month.

 Prices were up 3.4% from a year earlier, holding steady from July. ⛽️ Gas prices jumped 3.9% in August, accounting for more than a third of the overall monthly increase. Energy prices rose 2.1%.

 Core inflation also accelerated, with shelter costs gaining 0.3% after a 0.1% increase in July. Airfares rose 2.7%, while vehicle prices were also up. 🏥 Yes, but: Medical care prices fell 0.2%, and motor vehicle insurance dropped 0.8%. 

 The inflation data carries unusual weight ahead of next week's Fed meeting, where policymakers will consider the first interest rate hike in nearly three years.

 With less than two months before the midterms, renewed inflation pressures could also complicate the Trump administration's efforts to convince voters that affordability is improving. Go deeper . 

 
 
