 2. 📸 Photographers on 9/11 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Onlookers watch the World Trade Center burn on Sept. 11, 2001. Photo: Spencer Platt/Getty Images " This is history, this is history." 

 That's what photojournalist Spencer Platt was thinking 25 years ago as he photographed the events unfolding on the morning of 9/11. Platt and fellow Getty photographer Mario Tama told Axios' Sarah Grillo what it was like on the ground that day. Tama had just been hired by Getty Images two months before when he got the urgent call from his assignment editor. Rushing out the door of his Lower East Side apartment, he recalled the horror of what he was documenting:

 "There was definitely some cognitive dissonance — it was totally unfathomable. As I got closer on foot, I came across an office worker being treated on the ground, stunned and bloodied. Seeing blood coming out of his ear made the human toll of what was happening frighteningly real." Civilians take cover as a dust cloud from the collapse of the World Trade Center envelops Lower Manhattan on Sept. 11, 2001. Photo: Mario Tama/Getty Images While trying to reach Ground Zero, Tama saw the South Tower collapse:

 "I then heard a kind of shearing sound and looked up and, horrifyingly, I recall the tower tilted a bit and started to pancake down. In that moment, I went from being a photojournalist to any other New Yorker running for their life. It was absolutely terrifying." Keep reading . 

 
 
